**Answer: No, $C(X,I)$ is not compact in general.**

It suffices to give a single compact Hausdorff space $X$ for which $C(X,I)$ (with the topology of uniform convergence, i.e. the sup-norm topology) fails to be compact.

---

**Counterexample.** Take $X=[0,1]$, which is compact Hausdorff. Consider the sequence $(f_n)_{n\geq 1}$ in $C([0,1],[0,1])$ defined by
$$f_n(x)=x^n,\qquad x\in[0,1].$$
Each $f_n$ is continuous and $[0,1]$-valued, so $(f_n)\subset C(X,I)$.

**Pointwise limit.** For every $x\in[0,1)$ we have $x^n\to 0$, while $f_n(1)=1^n=1$ for all $n$. Hence $(f_n)$ converges pointwise to the function
$$g(x)=\begin{cases}0,&x\in[0,1),\\ 1,&x=1,\end{cases}$$
which is **not continuous** at $x=1$.

**No uniformly convergent subsequence.** Let $(f_{n_k})$ be any subsequence. Since $n_k\to\infty$, the same pointwise computation shows $f_{n_k}\to g$ pointwise. If $(f_{n_k})$ converged uniformly to some $h\in C([0,1],[0,1])$, then it would also converge pointwise to $h$; by uniqueness of the pointwise limit, $h=g$. But $h$ is continuous whereas $g$ is not — contradiction. Therefore **no subsequence of $(f_n)$ converges uniformly**.

**Conclusion.** $C([0,1],[0,1])$ with the sup-norm topology is a metric space, and in metric spaces compactness is equivalent to sequential compactness. The sequence $(f_n)$ has no convergent subsequence, so $C([0,1],[0,1])$ is not sequentially compact, hence **not compact**.

Since there exists a compact Hausdorff $X$ (namely $X=[0,1]$) for which $C(X,I)$ is not compact, the answer to the question is:

$$\boxed{C(X,I)\text{ is not compact in general.}}$$

---

**Remark (optional context).** The obstruction is precisely equicontinuity. By the Arzelà–Ascoli theorem, a subset of $C(X)$ is compact in the sup-norm topology iff it is closed, bounded, and equicontinuous. The set $C(X,I)$ is always closed and bounded, but it is equicontinuous only when $X$ is finite (equivalently, only when $C(X,I)\cong[0,1]^{|X|}$ is a finite product of compact intervals). For any infinite compact Hausdorff $X$ one can build a non-equicontinuous family in $C(X,I)$, so $C(X,I)$ is compact **iff $X$ is finite**. The example above is the canonical instance of this failure.

